window.docContents[136].resolve({"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/The-information-rates-re-dimension-to--T______/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--The-information-rates-re-dimension-to--T______":{"contents":"Per-character entropies are dimension one, but the per-second rates re-dimension to T⁻¹:\nthe average information rate, the average transinformation rate, the channel (time) capacity,\nthe bit and transfer rates, the call intensities. They collide there — distinct kinds, one\ndimension — just as the dimension-one quantities collide at the point one.\n\nThe bit rate is not the call intensity, though both are T⁻¹ (items 13-13, 13-7). There\nexist distinct IEC 80000-13 kinds with the same dimension T⁻¹ — the bit rate and the call\nintensity witness it, alongside the transfer and modulation rates and the information rates.\nDimension cannot separate them; the kind layer does. Uses the dimension\nmap.\n\niec80000_13_dim_collision, witnessed by ⟨binaryDigitRate, callIntensity, …⟩. The shared\ndimension is binaryDigitRate_dim_eq_callIntensity_dim; the kind distinction is a decide.\nAxiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","header":"31.3. The information rates re-dimension to  T⁻¹","id":"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/The-information-rates-re-dimension-to--T______/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--The-information-rates-re-dimension-to--T______"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"Show a PhysLib contributor, on one oscillator (a rigid point body of mass m on an ideal spring of stiffness k fixed to a wall, moving in one dimension), what each level of typing checks. With bare ℝ, Lean accepts every expression built from m and k, so √(m/k) type-checks where √(k/m) was meant. With PhysLib's WithDim, each expression has a dimension in its type, so an expression of the wrong dimension fails to type-check, but two quantities of equal dimension are interchangeable. With PKC kinds, each quantity also has a kind in its type, so quantities of equal dimension but different kind, such as angular frequency (rad/s) and frequency (Hz), cannot be added. The rest of the ladder builds on this distinction.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.1.1.1. Purpose","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Synopsis--Purpose"}});